  "worked_solution": "The curvature $\u03ba(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\n\u03ba(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{\u2032}(t){y}^{\u2033}(t)-{y}^{\u2032}(t){x}^{\u2033}(t)|}{([{x}^{\u2032}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{\u2032}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{\u03ba(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2{e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t) & =2{e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{\u2032}(t) & =2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}^{\u2033}(t) & =2{e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{\u2033}(t) & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u03ba(t) & =\\frac{|2{e}^{t}\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{t}|}{{((2{e}^{t})^{2}+(2{e}^{2t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{3t}}{{(4{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+{e}^{2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u03ba(0) & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+{e}^{2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{\u03ba(0)}=4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
